>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="law">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a gas safety certificate?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If you rent out a home with gas, yes. The law says every gas appliance you provide must be checked every 12 months by a Gas Safe registered engineer.</p>
      <h3>What landlords must do</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Get a new gas safety check every 12 months.</li>
        <li>Give your tenants a copy within 28 days of the check.</li>
        <li>Give new tenants a copy before they move in.</li>
        <li>Keep each record for at least 2 years.</li>
        <li>Fit a carbon monoxide alarm in any room with a gas boiler or other fixed fuel-burning appliance.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Keep your dates up to date.</strong> Missing a check is a criminal offence under the gas safety rules. We remind you before each certificate runs out.</p></div>
      <p>Home owners do not need a certificate by law. But a yearly check is a good idea, and many buyers ask for one when you sell.</p>
      <h3>What happens if a landlord does not have a gas safety certificate?</h3>
      <p>Not having a valid gas safety certificate is a criminal offence. The Health and Safety Executive (HSE) can prosecute, and the courts can give fines or, in serious cases, prison. It also puts your tenants at risk of a gas leak or carbon monoxide. It can cause problems with your insurance and with ending a tenancy too.</p>
      <h3>What if my certificate has run out?</h3>
      <p>Book a check straight away. Call <a href="tel:01515580334">0151 558 0334</a> and we will fit you in as soon as we can. Keep a record of the date you booked.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="checks">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What do you check?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We check every gas appliance in the home, the pipework and the air supply. The aim is simple: make sure nothing can leak gas or carbon monoxide.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>The gas supply</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>A tightness test to prove there are no gas leaks</li>
            <li>The gas pressure at the meter and at each appliance</li>
            <li>The emergency control valve works</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Each appliance</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>It burns gas safely, with a flue gas test on the boiler</li>
            <li>The flue takes fumes outside</li>
            <li>There is enough fresh air</li>
            <li>Safety devices cut the gas off if they should</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">We also test the carbon monoxide alarm. Our certificate covers boilers, gas cookers and hobs. It does not cover gas fires.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does it work?</h2>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Call or book online.</strong> Tell us the address and the gas appliances. We give you a fixed price.</li>
        <li><strong>We arrange access.</strong> We agree a time with you, your tenant or your agent.</li>
        <li><strong>The check.</strong> A Gas Safe registered engineer checks everything. Most homes take under an hour.</li>
        <li><strong>Your certificate.</strong> We email the certificate the same day, ready to give to your tenant.</li>
        <li><strong>Next year.</strong> We remind you before it runs out, so you never miss a date.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Need a certificate this week?</strong> Call and we will find the next free slot.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="fail">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What happens if something fails?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We tell you straight away and explain what it means. Gas engineers use three labels, set by the gas industry.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table stk">
          <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>What it means</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Label"><strong>Immediately dangerous</strong></td><td data-label="What it means">It is a risk to life right now.</td><td data-label="What happens">With your consent, we disconnect it from the gas. It must not be used until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Label"><strong>At risk</strong></td><td data-label="What it means">One or more faults could become dangerous.</td><td data-label="What happens">With your consent, we turn it off. It should not be used until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Label"><strong>Not to current standards</strong></td><td data-label="What it means">It meets older rules, but not today's.</td><td data-label="What happens">It can stay in use. We note it on the certificate.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If a repair is needed, we give you the price first. Often we can fix it on the same visit. Once everything is safe, we issue the certificate.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="vs-service">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Gas safety certificate or boiler service?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">They are two different jobs. A landlord needs the certificate by law. The service keeps the boiler working and its warranty valid.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table cmp3">
          <thead><tr><th>Compare</th><th>Gas safety certificate</th><th>Boiler service</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Compare"><strong>Checks</strong></td><td data-label="Gas safety certificate">Every gas appliance is safe</td><td data-label="Boiler service">The boiler inside: cleaned, tested, set up</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Compare"><strong>Needed by law?</strong></td><td data-label="Gas safety certificate">Yes, for landlords</td><td data-label="Boiler service">No, but most warranties need it</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Compare"><strong>Price</strong></td><td data-label="Gas safety certificate">£100</td><td data-label="Boiler service">£120</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Book both in one visit for <strong>£199</strong> and save £21. See our <a href="/boiler-service-liverpool">boiler service</a> page for what the service includes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="landlords">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Landlords, HMOs and letting agents</h2>
      <h3>HMOs in Liverpool</h3>
      <p>Liverpool City Council expects a valid gas safety certificate for every HMO licence. HMOs often have a gas hob in a shared kitchen as well as the boiler. We check them all in one visit and list each one on the certificate.</p>
      <h3>Portfolio landlords and agents</h3>
      <p>With 3 or more properties, each certificate is £80. You get one contact, and we track every renewal date for you. We can work straight with your letting agent and tenants.</p>
      <h3>Buying or selling</h3>
      <p>A gas safety check before you sell can answer a buyer's questions early. If you are buying, it tells you the boiler and gas supply are safe before you move in.</p>
      <div class="brx-note">
        <p><strong>Landlords also need an EICR.</strong> A gas safety certificate covers the gas. The electrics need an EICR at least every 5 years. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/eicr-liverpool">Ideal Electrical Solutions does landlord EICRs</a> from £149 including VAT, NICEIC approved, with the certificate emailed within 24 hours.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
